38409004,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the image of the line \\( y = 2x + 1 \\) under the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we first need to determine the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\). Given that \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) transforms \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can express \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFrom the given transformations, we have the following system of equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the following equations:\n\n1. \\(2a - b = 9\\)\n2. \\(2c - d = 3\\)\n3. \\(a - 3b = 7\\)\n4. \\(c - 3d = -1\\)\n\nWe solve the first two equations for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\nFrom equation (1): \\(b = 2a - 9\\)\nFrom equation (3): \\(b = \\frac{a - 7}{3}\\)\n\nEquating the two expressions for \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\n2a - 9 = \\frac{a - 7}{3}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 3:\n\n\\[\n6a - 27 = a - 7\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms:\n\n\\[\n5a = 20 \\implies a = 4\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 4\\) back into \\(b = 2a - 9\\):\n\n\\[\nb = 2(4) - 9 = 8 - 9 = -1\n\\]\n\nNow we solve the last two equations for \\(c\\) and \\(d\\):\n\nFrom equation (2): \\(d = 2c - 3\\)\nFrom equation (4): \\(d = c + 1\\)\n\nEquating the two expressions for \\(d\\):\n\n\\[\n2c - 3 = c + 1\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms:\n\n\\[\nc = 4\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(c = 4\\) back into \\(d = c + 1\\):\n\n\\[\nd = 4 + 1 = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\). Any point \\((x, y)\\) on the line can be written as \\((x, 2x + 1)\\). The transformation of this point under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 4x + 5(2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 14x + 5 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nLet the image point be \\((X, Y)\\). Then:\n\n\\[\nX = 2x - 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Y = 14x + 5\n\\]\n\nWe solve for \\(x\\) in terms of \\(X\\):\n\n\\[\nX = 2x - 1 \\implies 2x = X + 1 \\implies x = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(x = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\\) into the equation for \\(Y\\):\n\n\\[\nY = 14 \\left( \\frac{X + 1}{2} \\right) + 5 = 7(X + 1) + 5 = 7X + 7 + 5 = 7X + 12\n\\]\n\nThus, the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 7x + 12}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{y = 7x + 12}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:243"}
